WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.629
- I feel like a badass bitch
when I wear my hijab. (laughs)

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:06.212
(upbeat music)

00:00:10.283 --> 00:00:12.410
- Hijab is not just a piece of cloth

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:13.764
that we put on our head.

00:00:13.764 --> 00:00:15.711
- It's a symbol, to me, about being seen

00:00:15.711 --> 00:00:17.753
for your intelligence, before your beauty.

00:00:17.753 --> 00:00:20.994
- Wearing the hijab has
made me unapologetic

00:00:20.994 --> 00:00:22.718
about who I am, as a person.

00:00:22.718 --> 00:00:24.781
- When I started wearing
hijab, I was scared

00:00:24.781 --> 00:00:26.447
of like, "What's this person gonna say?"

00:00:26.447 --> 00:00:28.567
I went through 21 years of not wearing it.

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:30.335
- Once I came to that realization,

00:00:30.335 --> 00:00:32.719
that I think that wearing the hijab will

00:00:32.719 --> 00:00:36.228
make me a better person,
and hopefully help me

00:00:36.228 --> 00:00:38.900
become the kind of person
I want to be in the future.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.828
That's when I was like,
"Nope, I need to do this."

00:00:41.828 --> 00:00:44.018
So I went in, the first
day of sophomore year

00:00:44.018 --> 00:00:45.603
of high school, with, I remember,

00:00:45.603 --> 00:00:47.585
it was like a bright pink hijab. (laughs)

00:00:47.585 --> 00:00:49.414
- I was actually
encouraged by other people,

00:00:49.414 --> 00:00:51.442
and relatives, to take
it off, and not wear it.

00:00:51.442 --> 00:00:53.732
So, it was my choice to put it on,

00:00:53.732 --> 00:00:57.245
and it was my decision to
practice this part of my faith.

00:00:57.245 --> 00:00:58.936
- Well, my mother was very much against me

00:00:58.936 --> 00:01:01.476
wearing the hijab, because
my parents are both Muslim,

00:01:01.476 --> 00:01:05.022
but they're from Iran, and
over there, wearing the hijab

00:01:05.022 --> 00:01:07.422
is something that is
enforced among everybody.

00:01:07.422 --> 00:01:08.735
It is a political tool.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:10.508
Well, I put my religious
beliefs out there,

00:01:10.508 --> 00:01:11.887
and possibly be harmed.

00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:14.102
- It's unfortunate, that people have

00:01:14.102 --> 00:01:16.553
this negative opinion about the hijab.

00:01:16.553 --> 00:01:19.695
People who think that the
hijab oppresses women,

00:01:19.695 --> 00:01:22.028
but I hope to be an example,

00:01:23.445 --> 00:01:26.643
that I'm not an oppressed
woman who wears the hijab.

00:01:26.643 --> 00:01:29.118
- I had to keep explaining
that this is not about

00:01:29.118 --> 00:01:31.167
trying to prove what a good Muslim I am.

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:33.489
Whether you wear hijab or
not is between you and God,

00:01:33.489 --> 00:01:34.829
it is no one's place to judge.

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:37.522
I don't care if you're
a stripper at night,

00:01:37.522 --> 00:01:38.689
and you wear hijab during the day.

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:41.641
That is your business, you wear
hijab on your terms, honey,

00:01:41.641 --> 00:01:43.053
and that's what I do.

00:01:43.053 --> 00:01:45.627
- When I go up on stage at
open mics, and I perform,

00:01:45.627 --> 00:01:48.142
I definitely shock people
because I'm the only one

00:01:48.142 --> 00:01:51.028
wearing hijab, that gets up
on these public open mics.

00:01:51.028 --> 00:01:53.692
And they have the stereotypical
thing, when I walk in,

00:01:53.692 --> 00:01:55.578
like, "Oh, what is that
person doing here?"

00:01:55.578 --> 00:01:57.596
And then, all of a sudden,
I'm up there on stage,

00:01:57.596 --> 00:01:59.430
and I'm performing about a breakup,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.829
or a relationship, or death, or divorce,

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:04.642
or things like that, and people
are like, "Oh, she's human."

00:02:04.642 --> 00:02:06.476
It's like, "Surprise! We are."

00:02:06.476 --> 00:02:10.975
- I have changed the way I
look, to represent who I am.

00:02:10.975 --> 00:02:13.711
And because my identity is so out there,

00:02:13.711 --> 00:02:16.703
and I have to embrace it,
and I have to embrace that,

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:20.249
yes, sometimes, I'm going to
be looked at and get questions.

00:02:20.249 --> 00:02:23.727
You have no choice but to
become more confident about it,

00:02:23.727 --> 00:02:26.113
because you are wearing your
identity on your sleeve.

00:02:26.113 --> 00:02:29.522
- I do identify myself as a
feminist, and so I do write

00:02:29.522 --> 00:02:31.971
a lot about social issues,
and cultural issues,

00:02:31.971 --> 00:02:33.804
that transcend all these barriers.

00:02:33.804 --> 00:02:36.109
And the fact that I'm speaking
up about these things,

00:02:36.109 --> 00:02:38.906
in a hijab, it breaks
that stereotype, too,

00:02:38.906 --> 00:02:42.210
that we're the introverted,
domesticated, submissive

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.900
kind of women, when, no.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.725
We have a lot of outspoken,
very strong independent females,

00:02:47.725 --> 00:02:49.759
who do practice this part of the faith.

00:02:49.759 --> 00:02:53.604
- It's awesome that earlier
this year, Ibtihaj Muhammad

00:02:53.604 --> 00:02:57.714
went to the Olympics, wearing
the hijab, as a fencer there,

00:02:57.714 --> 00:02:59.542
representing the US team.

00:02:59.542 --> 00:03:03.218
And it just goes to show,
again, that she wasn't there

00:03:03.218 --> 00:03:04.618
because she wore her hijab.

00:03:04.618 --> 00:03:06.942
She was there because she
is good at what she does.

00:03:06.942 --> 00:03:09.814
- And honestly, whenever I
see any Muslim woman in hijab

00:03:09.814 --> 00:03:13.891
doing amazing things, making
it to magazine covers,

00:03:13.891 --> 00:03:16.876
and succeeding in different
elements of cooking,

00:03:16.876 --> 00:03:20.348
or baking, or fashion,
or business, in finance,

00:03:20.348 --> 00:03:22.778
it's a huge empowering feeling.

00:03:22.778 --> 00:03:24.854
It's an inspiration, you
see it, and you feel like,

00:03:24.854 --> 00:03:26.772
"Yeah, someone else
could do it, so can I."

00:03:26.772 --> 00:03:28.330
So I'm going to keep doing what I do,

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.178
practicing my faith the way that I am.

00:03:30.178 --> 00:03:32.995
- I see women sexualized to the core,

00:03:32.995 --> 00:03:36.411
I see women having to feel
validation from how they look,

00:03:36.411 --> 00:03:39.312
and I have struggled through
trying to seek that validation

00:03:39.312 --> 00:03:40.767
through how I look, as well.

00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:43.636
And to cover my hair, and
to have everything covered,

00:03:43.636 --> 00:03:46.913
to me, is a symbol of, "I
want nothing to do with that,

00:03:46.913 --> 00:03:50.385
"for myself, and I am
taking my beauty back."

00:03:50.385 --> 00:03:52.593
- With the rise of social
media, you see that

00:03:52.593 --> 00:03:55.473
there's not just one way for a woman

00:03:55.473 --> 00:03:56.750
who wears hijab to look.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.702
You can have so many different styles.

00:03:58.702 --> 00:04:02.302
- Having that opportunity to
do fun stuff with our hijabs

00:04:02.302 --> 00:04:04.318
is really nice, to be able to add colors,

00:04:04.318 --> 00:04:06.880
and add sparkles, and
wrap it certain ways,

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.442
so that we get to express
our feelings of beauty,

00:04:10.442 --> 00:04:12.466
in a certain way, as well.

00:04:12.466 --> 00:04:14.966
(happy music)

